{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_102","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat > agent/report.md <<'EOF'\n# Calibrating price paths with seasonal volatility and uncertainty\n\n## Context\nSynth scores a distribution of price paths, not a point forecast. This campaign optimizes the official validator CRPS across 24-hour crypto, 24-hour commodity/equity, and 1-hour crypto configurations. All market observations come from the frozen snapshot ending September 14, 2026. The starter uses seven-day minute-return standard deviation with independent Gaussian increments.\n\n## Approach\nThe research isolates seasonality, scale estimation, volatility forecasting and uncertainty in future volatility. Seasonal profiles are fitted before validation. Runtime calibration uses only the preceding context, leaves missing prices unfilled, and respects prompt issuance delays. All variants honor the requested path count and simulation grid. Randomness is controlled by SYNTH_BENCHMARK_SEED.\n\nThe leading candidate combines a heterogeneous autoregression (HAR) of absolute returns with deterministic intraday/weekly volatility profiles and a shared lognormal volatility multiplier per path. Antithetic shocks reduce sampling noise. A robust half-hour session profile improves the commodity/equity group. Final model selection and confirmation remain in progress.\n\n## Experiments\nTwo prespecified development weeks, August 1–7 and August 17–23, expose substantially different crypto volatility regimes. Each has 28 prompts per asset/format at 1000 paths. Scores are compared on identical realized observations and coverage. CRPS is computed by synth_lib.backtester.scoring._score_single_prompt through agent/predict.py; the metric is not reimplemented.\n\nThe supplied offline score bundle was absent, so the direct official scoring routine is used for research. This yields valid CRPS comparisons without a field-relative ranking. The installed library gates the volatility component after the snapshot cutoff; current-rule replays explicitly apply the published formula to historical observations. Historical-formula 1h scores are also saved separately.\n\nSeasonality delivers the largest improvement for commodity/equity assets. Resampling alone explains little of it. Absolute returns resist isolated spikes better than squared returns. Faster volatility adaptation helps the calm week but sometimes overreacts in the volatile week. A learned point forecast alone is not uniformly superior, but combining it with moderate uncertainty in future scale improves results. Excessively wide scale mixtures lose precision. Conservatively fitted return drift fails out of sample despite positive training R².\n\n## Results\nPreliminary results only; final confirmation pending. In configuration order COM_EQU_24H / CRYPTO_24H / CRYPTO_1H:\n\n| Model | August 1–7 | August 17–23 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Starter | 3307.692 / 2831.101 / 629.151 | 2204.847 / 6688.765 / 1215.723 |\n| HAR, hourly profile, mixture0.4 | 2984.519 / 2752.661 / 618.427 | 2006.982 / 6549.743 / 1183.802 |\n| HAR, robust half-hour profile, mixture0.4 | 2969.848 / 2750.012 / 618.170 | 1991.558 / 6552.466 / 1180.774 |\n\nConfiguration values are equal-weight averages of per-asset mean CRPS, not field ranks or reconstructed rewards. Cross-configuration raw CRPS magnitudes are not directly comparable. Prompt-level measurements and experiment metadata are saved in results/.\n\n## Limitations\nDevelopment prompts overlap and are not statistically independent. Hyperparameter search can overfit two weeks; separate confirmation and seed checks are required. Missing realized values reduce absolute CRPS, so model comparisons use identical coverage. Seasonality is venue-specific, and extraordinary event schedules can depart from normal weekday patterns. No claim about future September 15–24 performance is possible from the permitted data.\n\n## With more budget\nPending completion of the ongoing nonlinear volatility, denser-grid, seed and chronological confirmation experiments.\nEOF\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_102","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat > agent/report.md <<'EOF'\n# Calibrating price paths with seasonal volatility and uncertainty\n\n## Context\nSynth scores a distribution of price paths, not a point forecast. This campaign optimizes the official validator CRPS across 24-hour crypto, 24-hour commodity/equity, and 1-hour crypto configurations. All market observations come from the frozen snapshot ending September 14, 2026. The starter uses seven-day minute-return standard deviation with independent Gaussian increments.\n\n## Approach\nThe research isolates seasonality, scale estimation, volatility forecasting and uncertainty in future volatility. Seasonal profiles are fitted before validation. Runtime calibration uses only the preceding context, leaves missing prices unfilled, and respects prompt issuance delays. All variants honor the requested path count and simulation grid. Randomness is controlled by SYNTH_BENCHMARK_SEED.\n\nThe leading candidate combines a heterogeneous autoregression (HAR) of absolute returns with deterministic intraday/weekly volatility profiles and a shared lognormal volatility multiplier per path. Antithetic shocks reduce sampling noise. A robust half-hour session profile improves the commodity/equity group. Final model selection and confirmation remain in progress.\n\n## Experiments\nTwo prespecified development weeks, August 1–7 and August 17–23, expose substantially different crypto volatility regimes. Each has 28 prompts per asset/format at 1000 paths. Scores are compared on identical realized observations and coverage. CRPS is computed by synth_lib.backtester.scoring._score_single_prompt through agent/predict.py; the metric is not reimplemented.\n\nThe supplied offline score bundle was absent, so the direct official scoring routine is used for research. This yields valid CRPS comparisons without a field-relative ranking. The installed library gates the volatility component after the snapshot cutoff; current-rule replays explicitly apply the published formula to historical observations. Historical-formula 1h scores are also saved separately.\n\nSeasonality delivers the largest improvement for commodity/equity assets. Resampling alone explains little of it. Absolute returns resist isolated spikes better than squared returns. Faster volatility adaptation helps the calm week but sometimes overreacts in the volatile week. A learned point forecast alone is not uniformly superior, but combining it with moderate uncertainty in future scale improves results. Excessively wide scale mixtures lose precision. Conservatively fitted return drift fails out of sample despite positive training R².\n\n## Results\nPreliminary results only; final confirmation pending. In configuration order COM_EQU_24H / CRYPTO_24H / CRYPTO_1H:\n\n| Model | August 1–7 | August 17–23 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Starter | 3307.692 / 2831.101 / 629.151 | 2204.847 / 6688.765 / 1215.723 |\n| HAR, hourly profile, mixture0.4 | 2984.519 / 2752.661 / 618.427 | 2006.982 / 6549.743 / 1183.802 |\n| HAR, robust half-hour profile, mixture0.4 | 2969.848 / 2750.012 / 618.170 | 1991.558 / 6552.466 / 1180.774 |\n\nConfiguration values are equal-weight averages of per-asset mean CRPS, not field ranks or reconstructed rewards. Cross-configuration raw CRPS magnitudes are not directly comparable. Prompt-level measurements and experiment metadata are saved in results/.\n\n## Limitations\nDevelopment prompts overlap and are not statistically independent. Hyperparameter search can overfit two weeks; separate confirmation and seed checks are required. Missing realized values reduce absolute CRPS, so model comparisons use identical coverage. Seasonality is venue-specific, and extraordinary event schedules can depart from normal weekday patterns. No claim about future September 15–24 performance is possible from the permitted data.\n\n## With more budget\nPending completion of the ongoing nonlinear volatility, denser-grid, seed and chronological confirmation experiments.\nEOF\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_103","type":"agent_message","text":"The drift forecast failed the out-of-sample test and is being discarded. Combining the learned volatility model with robust half-hour seasonality improved commodity/equity CRPS by about 10% versus the starter on both development weeks. A small nonlinear volatility model has finished training; its training fit looks stronger, but the next CRPS runs will determine whether that transfers out of sample."}}
